<Docs edit="site/src/pages/help/versions.astro" title="Versions" description="What Facet's version numbers mean, what 1.0 needs, and the Release, Beta and Dev update channels." group="Help" current={url('/help/versions/')} headings={headings}>
  <h2 id="beta">Facet is in beta</h2>
  <p>
    While its version starts with 0 (0.5, 0.6 and on), Facet as a whole is a beta product: it works and people run it
    every day, but screens and settings still change between versions, and some rough edges are known.
  </p>

  <h2 id="numbers">Version numbers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>0.6, 0.7, 0.8…</strong>: feature releases, one at a time, each with a goal.</li>
    <li><strong>0.6.1, 0.6.2…</strong>: fixes only, between feature releases, and only when something can't wait.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>0.6.0-beta.1, 0.6.0-beta.2…</strong>: test builds of the next version on the Beta channel, before it
      ships. When testing is done, the finished 0.6.0 comes out as a release.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The first plain release is 0.5.0. It closes the 0.5 betas, and a panel on 0.5.0-beta.10 sees it as an update.
  </p>

  <h2 id="one">What 1.0 needs</h2>
  <p>
    Facet becomes 1.0 when the <a href={url('/editor/')}>editor</a> and config backup are done, every test passes, and
    there have been a few weeks without a nasty bug: when it can be given to a stranger with no caveats. After 1.0 the
    pattern stays the same (1.1 for features, 1.0.1 for fixes); 2.0 would only come if your Homes or settings had to
    be set up again.
  </p>

  <h2 id="channels">Update channels</h2>
  <p>
    The panel's <strong>Update channel</strong>, in its Settings or as a select on its device page in Home Assistant,
    picks which updates it is offered:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Release</strong> (the default): every finished version (0.5.0, 0.6.0, 0.6.1).</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Beta</strong>: where you can try the next version's screens and features before it ships, without being
      on nightly builds. Its builds are test versions of the next release (0.6.0-beta.1, 0.6.0-beta.2) with short
      notes. Beta gets every release too, so it is never behind Release.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dev (nightly)</strong>: a build of the latest code every night when something changed. It has passed the
      automatic checks but nobody has tried it on a panel, so it can break things.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A panel set to <strong>Stable</strong> (the old name) is on Release. A panel is only offered versions newer than
    the one it runs, so after moving from Dev back to Beta or Release it updates with that channel's next version.
    The <a href={url('/install/')}>Install</a> page offers Release and Beta.
  </p>

  <h2 id="yours">Your version, and updating</h2>
  <p>
    The panel's <strong>Settings &gt; Update</strong> row says the version installed, and so does the panel's device
    page in Home Assistant. When a newer one is out on the panel's channel, Home Assistant shows a
    <strong>Firmware</strong> update with its release notes; press <strong>Install</strong> when it suits you.
    <a href={url('/help/troubleshooting/#updates')}>Updating and troubleshooting</a> has the details.
  </p>

  <h2 id="plans">What's coming</h2>
  <p>
    Each version is a <a href={`${REPO_URL}/milestones`}>milestone on GitHub</a>, named after it, with its goal and
    the issues it will close. The <a href="https://github.com/users/mrdarrengriffin/projects/3">project board</a> shows
    everything planned{UPDATES_PAGE && <>, and <a href={url('/updates/')}>Updates</a> what is being built now and what
    each release brought</>}.
  </p>
</Docs>
